\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}1,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f519fa9-de89-5b00-aa02-312cf8e32cba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3d8a45-fe13-581e-9e3f-055e9482e906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-10,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482865} {:answer/id #uuid "2eee8041-f4fa-545a-a3f7-4d63c35b6ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-10,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482892} {:answer/id #uuid "3dacba62-f7cd-5fb4-b7ac-b6a55cdc2a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-10,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482917} {:answer/id #uuid "982b05bc-9e84-5fbb-95ab-465cc3b39a18" :answer/type {:db/